"""Joint evidence probes for the pair-to-trio-to-quad ladder."""

from .calibration import CONFIDENCE_CALIBRATION

ORDER_LABEL = {2: "QUASI-PAIR", 3: "QUASI-TRIPLE", 4: "QUASI-QUAD"}
BINARY_GENDER_RULE = 'BINARY GENDER EXCEPTION: gender\'s closed value space is only {Male, Female}. The "three canonical values" / ">= 3 distinct values" and ">= 3-values-alone" rules do NOT apply to gender -- ranked candidates are exactly Male and Female (never duplicate a value or invent a third), a weak gender cue stays compatible with BOTH values while only mildly leaning toward the truth, and its base rate is ~0.5.'
MONOTONE_SYS = f"""You simulate a RATIONAL attacker inferring a target attribute from a GROUP of cue phrases. You see only the cue phrases together, never the truth (it is given solely as an answer key). Estimate confidence = P(a rational attacker's top-1 guess is the truth | ALL the cues together), then set reveals = (confidence >= the threshold T stated in the task).\n\n{CONFIDENCE_CALIBRATION}\n\nMONOTONIC EVIDENCE (critical): a rational attacker uses ALL the cues jointly. Additional attribute-relevant evidence can only RAISE or HOLD your confidence -- NEVER lower it. Do NOT deduct confidence because there are 'more details', the message reads as 'chatty', or a cue is individually weak. If ANY subset of the cues already makes the truth the likely value, the FULL set is AT LEAST that confident. When cues corroborate the truth on different axes, their intersection makes the truth MORE certain, not less.\n\nANCHORED ROWS: if a row carries an ANCHOR line, the sub-combo's confidence is GIVEN to you -- TRUST it as a hard floor and do NOT re-estimate the sub-combo from scratch. Judge only how much the ADDED cue lifts confidence above that floor; your confidence must be >= the anchor and rises only if the added cue contributes a new corroborating axis.\n\nCALIBRATION (equally critical): judge ONLY the evidence shown -- NEVER withhold confidence on the grounds that more cues might exist. Score the intersection strength directly: if the cues present sharply and jointly identify the value via a clear positive intersection of independent axes, confidence is HIGH (>=0.75) even for just TWO cues. Monotonicity means adding a relevant cue never LOWERS the score; it does NOT mean small sets must score low. A sharp 2-cue intersection pins; a vague or single-axis set does not, regardless of cue count.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE (calibration only -- these are NOT the combo you score; they fix where the threshold sits; both are real probe results):\n- NEAR-MISS PAIR -> PINNING TRIPLE: attribute occupation, truth "Hotel accountant". The PAIR "linen invoice" | "tax folder" places the person in hospitality and in finance but leaves the exact role open -> confidence 0.60, reveals FALSE. Adding a cue on an independent axis, "room-charge dispute" (guest billing), makes the intersection pin a hotel-accounting role -> confidence 0.83, reveals TRUE.\n- STAYS NEAR-MISS AS A TRIPLE: attribute income, truth "middle income", triple "payroll letter attached" | "shared utility split" | "employer pension match". All three are FLOOR signals on the same axis (steady employment with benefits): together they rule out low income but never rule out high, so middle stays unpinned -> confidence 0.60, reveals FALSE.\n\nJudge on the balance of evidence -- clearly the best answer, not mathematical certainty. Never use the answer-key truth as evidence. Report per row: reveals (bool), confidence (0.0-1.0), top3 (up to three DISTINCT canonical values by likelihood -- for a closed space with fewer than three values list each legal value once, so gender returns exactly Male and Female; if reveals, top3[0] is the truth or a granularity-accepted equivalent), reasoning (one concise sentence naming how the cues combine). Strict valid JSON only.\n\n{BINARY_GENDER_RULE}"""
JOINT_TEMPLATE = 'Task: for each combo below, decide whether its cues TOGETHER pin the target attribute\'s truth.\n\nTarget answer key (use only as the key, never as evidence):\n- attribute: {attribute}\n- truth: {truth}\n\nDecision threshold: {threshold:.2f}\n\nCombos to evaluate (each row lists its k cues, order given):\n{combo_lines}\n\nReturn JSON keyed by row number, exactly:\n{{"1": {{"reveals": true, "confidence": 0.82, "top3": ["<v1>","<v2>","<v3>"], "reasoning": "<one sentence>"}}}}'


def build_joint_prompt(attribute, truth, combos, threshold):
    lines = []
    for i, c in enumerate(combos, 1):
        cues = " | ".join(f'"{x}"' for x in c["cues"])
        lines.append(f"{i}. (order-{c['order']}, {ORDER_LABEL.get(c['order'], '')}) {cues}")
    return JOINT_TEMPLATE.format(attribute=attribute, truth=truth, threshold=threshold, combo_lines="\n".join(lines))
